Content blocker: EasyList-driven blocking, user-managed lists, Settings.

Blocks advertising and tracking requests before they are sent, using filter
lists the user controls: three built in, any number added by URL, each one
switchable, all refreshable on demand.

The engine is Chromium's, not ours

  components/subresource_filter already blocks subresources before the
  request leaves the browser, memory-maps a flatbuffer ruleset shared by
  every renderer, and since 152 applies cosmetic rules through
  style_rule_matcher. components/subresource_filter/tools/rule_parser
  describes itself, verbatim, as "A parser of EasyList rules". Both are
  ordinary source_set/static_library targets with no testonly marker - which
  was worth checking, because had they been host-tool-only, lists would have
  had to be baked in at build time and user-managed lists would have been
  impossible.

  What upstream has no notion of is a filter list somebody chose. That gap
  is all this fills:

    list text -> RuleParser -> proto::UrlRule / proto::StyleRule
              -> UnindexedRulesetWriter -> one merged unindexed file
              -> RulesetService::IndexAndStoreAndPublishRulesetIfNeeded()

  and the existing machinery indexes it, verifies its checksum, versions it
  on disk and publishes it to every renderer. No matching code is Aerium's.

Chrome's RulesetService, not a second one

  RulesetConfig allows several to coexist, and the first draft used that.
  It was wrong: subresource_filter's renderer half is singular, and 152
  ships no second ruleset anywhere in the tree, so a parallel publish path
  would be ours to maintain against upstream indefinitely. Reusing the
  existing service costs nothing - that slot holds Safe Browsing's ad filter
  upstream, and ungoogled-chromium removes Safe Browsing, so it is never
  populated in an Aerium build. An empty slot, not a working feature.

Activation

  Upstream runs the filter only where a Safe Browsing verdict says so.
  Aerium blocks everywhere, and that decision cannot come from a pref either
  because the throttle is in components/ while the pref is chrome/'s
  local_state. A process-global set by the service bridges it, read and
  written on the UI thread only. The throttle then takes the same route
  DevTools already uses - set the level, record FORCED_ACTIVATION - so
  metrics and disabled_reason bookkeeping see a state they understand, and
  sits before the DevTools check so debugging keeps the last word.

No circular GN dependency

  Nothing in //chrome/browser/aerium_blocker includes browser_process.h.
  //chrome/browser depends on that target, so an include back into it is a
  circular dependency; Chromium supports those through
  allow_circular_includes_from, but the documented cost is a build graph
  that misses generated dependencies and fails at compile time - an
  expensive lesson thirteen hours into a build. The service takes a
  RulesetServiceGetter callback and its instance is created explicitly from
  ChromeBrowserMainParts::PostBrowserStart(); the Settings handler reads
  lists from the service rather than from local_state.

Where it lives: Aerium Guard, not Privacy and security

  The shield in the address bar gains a "Block ads and trackers" checkbox
  above the three modes, because that is what people open the shield for.
  The full list management - toggles, add, remove, Update now, rule counts -
  lives on the Aerium Guard settings page.

  The checkbox is a checkbox and not a fourth mode, and blocking is
  deliberately not part of PresetValues. The three modes are mutually
  exclusive descriptions of how the browser is set up; blocking is orthogonal
  and every mode wants it on. Folding it in would mean switching the ad
  blocker off silently renamed the active mode to "Custom" - a control with a
  side effect on an unrelated label.

  Hosted in exactly one place, per the rule the Guard page already states
  about delete-on-exit: showing the same pref-bound controls in two sections
  gives the user two switches for one setting and no way to tell which one
  they last touched. The Guard page had no prefs before - it dealt only in
  modes, through its handler - so it gains a prefs binding from settings-main
  like every other page has.

  The section itself: an on/off toggle, an
  automatic-update toggle disclosing exactly what the fetch does and does
  not send, the list of filter lists with rule counts and per-list errors,
  add and remove, and Update now.

  It does not go through settings_private, although the configuration is a
  pref. Adding a list has to be validated and then fetched, which a pref
  write cannot express, and "Update now" is an action with no state to
  store - inventing one would be a pref that exists to be a button. The two
  bound toggles do use settings_private, which reaches local_state on its
  own through PrefsUtil::FindServiceForPref.

Lists

  Ten ship, and the defaults are uBlock Origin's rather than a set assembled
  here: uBlock filters, uBlock badware, uBlock privacy, EasyList, EasyPrivacy
  and Peter Lowe's server list. That is the most widely exercised blocking
  configuration in existence, tested against site breakage by millions of
  people daily; picking a different combination would mean discovering that
  breakage ourselves.

  AdGuard Base, AdGuard Tracking Protection, AdGuard Annoyances and EasyList
  Cookie ship present but switched off. They overlap EasyList heavily, so
  enabling them alongside it spends indexing time and memory on rules already
  there - but some people want them, and present-and-off puts the choice in
  reach without imposing the cost. EasyList Cookie is off for a different
  reason: hiding a cookie banner without answering it leaves the site's own
  default consent standing, which is not what switching it on suggests.

  Every one of the ten URLs was fetched and confirmed to serve filter-list
  content before being written down. A default list that 404s is a blocker
  that silently does less than it claims.

  All enabled lists merge into one ruleset, because the indexed ruleset is
  one flatbuffer and matching walks one index - a fourth list costs a
  re-index, not a fourth lookup per request. The content version hashes each
  enabled list's URL and bytes, so a refresh returning identical content
  skips indexing entirely.

  Custom lists are https-only. A list rewritten in flight is an instruction
  about what to block and, through allowlist rules, what to stop blocking.
  Fetches send no cookies, no credentials and no referrer: a daily fetch
  carrying identity is a beacon.

  Startup rebuilds from cached text before touching the network, so a launch
  offline blocks with last week's lists rather than with nothing. A failed
  fetch keeps the previous copy live and says so on the row. A fetch that
  yields zero usable rules reports that, rather than showing "0 rules" -
  which is indistinguishable from a list with nothing left to block.

Parsing untrusted text in the browser process

  Deliberate and bounded. RuleParser is pure string handling, allocates no
  attacker-sized buffers, and Chromium fuzzes it continuously
  (tools/rule_parser/rule_parser_fuzzer.cc). It is still privileged code
  reading remote input, so the parse is confined to one free function with a
  plain in/out contract: moving it behind a mojo interface later changes
  that function's body, not the design around it.
